The lecture’s contrast is sharp: $\begin{aligned}
\text{freedom} &\neq \text{doing what we want},\\
\text{freedom} &= \text{autonomy}.\end{aligned}$ And autonomy immediately requires an opposite: $\begin{aligned}
\text{autonomy} &= \text{acting according to a law I give myself},\\
\text{heteronomy} &= \text{acting according to an inclination or desire not self-given}.\end{aligned}$

