p.src:
The safest reconstruction, faithful to the visible wording while avoiding the omitted portions of the quotation, is $\begin{aligned}
\text{good merely as means} &\Longrightarrow \text{hypothetical imperative},\\
\text{good in itself} &\Longrightarrow \text{categorical imperative}.\end{aligned}$ This is the point at which the lecture’s pieces lock together. If morality requires duty rather than inclination, and if freedom requires autonomy rather than heteronomy, then the command appropriate to moral freedom must be categorical rather than hypothetical.

