p.src:
Sandel then turns to the second formulation because it brings the moral intuition into sharper focus. A categorical imperative cannot be grounded in merely private or contingent ends. Its ground must lie in something with absolute value. Kant’s answer is that rational beings exist as ends in themselves. The formula is: $\begin{aligned}
\text{Treat humanity, whether in yourself or another, never simply as a means}\\
\text{but always at the same time as an end.}
\end{aligned}$

